a-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.9177\\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186064506 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{25}$ be a random sample of size $n=25$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.15,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}>1.6).$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the sample size is sufficiently large for the central limit theorem to be applied. A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.1\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8790\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8810\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8830\\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.2\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8980\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.8997\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.9015\\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.3\\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.9147\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.9162\\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0.9177\\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186064888 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186064888 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186074180 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise about $O$ can be represented by\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(y,-x).\n$$\n\nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(5,6)↦(6,-5).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, applying the translation to $(6,-5),$ we get\n\n$$\n(6,-5)↦(6+k,\\,-5+k).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(12,1),$ by equating the $x$- and $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6+k=12 \\\\ -5+k=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,k & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=6.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186074180 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise about $O$ can be represented by\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(y,-x).\n$$\n\nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(5,6)↦(6,-5).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, applying the translation to $(6,-5),$ we get\n\n$$\n(6,-5)↦(6+k,\\,-5+k).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(12,1),$ by equating the $x$- and $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}6+k=12 \\\\ -5+k=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,k & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=6.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186074182 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA rotation of ${270}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about $O$ can be represented by \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(y,-x).\n$$\n\nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(-5,-2)↦(-2,5).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the translation to $(-2,5),$ we get\n\n$$\n(-2,5)↦(-2+p,5+q).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(2,-9),$ by equating the $x$- and $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2+p=2 \\\\ 5+q=-9\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p=4 \\\\ q=-14.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\np-q=4+(-14)=-10.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186074182 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA rotation of ${270}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about $O$ can be represented by \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(y,-x).\n$$\n\nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(-5,-2)↦(-2,5).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the translation to $(-2,5),$ we get\n\n$$\n(-2,5)↦(-2+p,5+q).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(2,-9),$ by equating the $x$- and $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-2+p=2 \\\\ 5+q=-9\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}p=4 \\\\ q=-14.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\np-q=4+(-14)=-10.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186078369 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (kx,ky)\\,.\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to $P',$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k\\cdot 1,k\\cdot 2)=(-2,-4)\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}k=-2 \\\\ 2k=-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\dfrac{(-4)}{2}=-2.$\n\n![](images/21/219980e6dee08094db21c153f37b107d6fd85709aa50cf8c546a5ba050aac567.png)" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186078369 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (kx,ky)\\,.\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to $P',$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k\\cdot 1,k\\cdot 2)=(-2,-4)\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}k=-2 \\\\ 2k=-4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\dfrac{(-4)}{2}=-2.$\n\n![](images/21/219980e6dee08094db21c153f37b107d6fd85709aa50cf8c546a5ba050aac567.png)" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186079012 1071 "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{b-3x}+2, & x\\le 0 \\\\ x+3, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=0$, find the value of the constant $b.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186079012 1071 "Given that the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{b-3x}+2, & x\\le 0 \\\\ x+3, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis continuous at $x=0$, find the value of the constant $b.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186079015 1071 "Find the value of the constant $a$ that makes the function $g(x)$ below continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2ax+1}, & x\\le 2 \\\\ 2x+5, & x>2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186079015 1071 "Find the value of the constant $a$ that makes the function $g(x)$ below continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2ax+1}, & x\\le 2 \\\\ 2x+5, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186083967 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $Y$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le y\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-6,-1],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5},\\, & -6\\le y\\le -1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>-5) & =P(-5<Y<-1) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-(-5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186083967 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $Y$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le y\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $Y$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-6,-1],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5},\\, & -6\\le y\\le -1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>-5) & =P(-5<Y<-1) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-(-5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186092600 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $a,b,$ and $d$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{a,b,d\\}.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186092600 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $a,b,$ and $d$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{a,b,d\\}.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [1759