$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.2p+0.3q=0.6 \\\\ 0.2p-0.1q=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186097149 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply both equations by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.2p+0.3q=0.6 & |\\times 10 \\\\ 0.2p-0.1q=0.4 & |\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+3q=6 \\\\ 2p-q=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $-2p$ term that can cancel with the $2p$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+3q=6 &  \\\\ 2p-q=4 & |\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+3q=6 \\\\ -2p+q=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $p$-variable and allows us to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{2p}+3q & =6 \\\\ -\\cancel{2p}+q & =-4 \\\\ \\hline (3q+q) & =(6-4) \\\\ 4q & =2 \\\\ q & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ q & =0.5\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $q=0.5$ into the equation $2p-q=4$ to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-q & =4 \\\\ 2p-0.5 & =4 \\\\ 2p & =4.5 \\\\ p & =\\frac{4.5}{2} \\\\ p & =2.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=2.25,\\,q=0.5.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186097149 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply both equations by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.2p+0.3q=0.6 & |\\times 10 \\\\ 0.2p-0.1q=0.4 & |\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+3q=6 \\\\ 2p-q=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $-2p$ term that can cancel with the $2p$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+3q=6 &  \\\\ 2p-q=4 & |\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+3q=6 \\\\ -2p+q=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $p$-variable and allows us to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{2p}+3q & =6 \\\\ -\\cancel{2p}+q & =-4 \\\\ \\hline (3q+q) & =(6-4) \\\\ 4q & =2 \\\\ q & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ q & =0.5\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $q=0.5$ into the equation $2p-q=4$ to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-q & =4 \\\\ 2p-0.5 & =4 \\\\ 2p & =4.5 \\\\ p & =\\frac{4.5}{2} \\\\ p & =2.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=2.25,\\,q=0.5.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186097319 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}p+q=1 \\\\ -2p-3q=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186097319 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}p+q=1 \\\\ -2p-3q=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186097319 1074 "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $5.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}p+q=1 & |\\,\\times 5 \\\\ -2p-3q=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+5q=5 \\\\ -2p-3q=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $2p$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-2p$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $p$-variable, and allows us to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{2p}+5q & =5 \\\\ -\\cancel{2p}-3q & =1 \\\\ \\hline 2q & =6 \\\\ q & =3\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $q=3$ into the equation $2p+5q=5$ to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p+5q & =5 \\\\ 2p+5\\cdot 3 & =5 \\\\ 2p+15 & =5 \\\\ 2p & =-10 \\\\ p & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-5$ and $q=3.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186097319 1074 "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $5.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}p+q=1 & |\\,\\times 5 \\\\ -2p-3q=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+5q=5 \\\\ -2p-3q=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $2p$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-2p$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $p$-variable, and allows us to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{2p}+5q & =5 \\\\ -\\cancel{2p}-3q & =1 \\\\ \\hline 2q & =6 \\\\ q & =3\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $q=3$ into the equation $2p+5q=5$ to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p+5q & =5 \\\\ 2p+5\\cdot 3 & =5 \\\\ 2p+15 & =5 \\\\ 2p & =-10 \\\\ p & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-5$ and $q=3.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186097810 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with decimal coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.2x+y=1 \\\\ 0.15x-0.04y=0.2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo make the system easier to solve, we turn it into a system involving integer coefficients only.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by $10,$ or $100,$ or $1000,$ etc. depending on how many decimal places we need to eliminate. In this particular instance:\n\n- We need to eliminate one decimal place in the top equation, so we multiply by $10.$\n\n- We need to eliminate two decimal places in the bottom equation, so we multiply by $100.$\n\nSo, we multiply the first equation by $10$ and the second equation by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{red}0.2} x + y = 1 & {\\color{blue}| \\times  10} \\\\ {\\color{red}0.15} x - {\\color{red}0.04} y = {\\color{red}0.2} & {\\color{blue}| \\times  100}\\end{array} \\right. }\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 10 y = 10 \\\\ 15 x - 4 y = 20\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can solve the system of equations using either substitution or elimination." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186097810 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with decimal coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.2x+y=1 \\\\ 0.15x-0.04y=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the system easier to solve, we turn it into a system involving integer coefficients only.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by $10,$ or $100,$ or $1000,$ etc. depending on how many decimal places we need to eliminate. In this particular instance:\n\n- We need to eliminate one decimal place in the top equation, so we multiply by $10.$\n\n- We need to eliminate two decimal places in the bottom equation, so we multiply by $100.$\n\nSo, we multiply the first equation by $10$ and the second equation by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\color{red}0.2} x + y = 1 & {\\color{blue}| \\times  10} \\\\ {\\color{red}0.15} x - {\\color{red}0.04} y = {\\color{red}0.2} & {\\color{blue}| \\times  100}\\end{array} \\right. }\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 10 y = 10 \\\\ 15 x - 4 y = 20\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can solve the system of equations using either substitution or elimination." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186097919 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with fractional coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y=-4 \\\\ 7x-5y=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can turn the system into an equivalent system involving only integer coefficients. This will make it easier to solve.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by its lowest common denominator. In this instance, multiplying the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions $(4)$ will do the job:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {\\color{red}2}}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{ {\\color{red}4}}} y = - 4 & {\\color{blue}| \\hspace{0.222em} \\times  4} \\\\ 7 x - 5 y = - 2 & \\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 5 y = - 16 \\\\ 7 x - 5 y = - 2\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the system using, for example, the elimination method. Notice that the $5y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-5y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}2x+\\cancel{5y} & =-16 \\\\ 7x-\\cancel{5y} & =-2 \\\\ \\hline 9x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $7x - 5y= -2$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-5y & =-2 \\\\ 7(-2)-5y & =-2 \\\\ -14-5y & =-2 \\\\ -5y & =12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2, \\,y=-\\dfrac{12}{5}.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186097919 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with fractional coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y=-4 \\\\ 7x-5y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can turn the system into an equivalent system involving only integer coefficients. This will make it easier to solve.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by its lowest common denominator. In this instance, multiplying the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions $(4)$ will do the job:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {\\color{red}2}}} x + {\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{ {\\color{red}4}}} y = - 4 & {\\color{blue}| \\hspace{0.222em} \\times  4} \\\\ 7 x - 5 y = - 2 & \\end{array} \\right. }\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}2 x + 5 y = - 16 \\\\ 7 x - 5 y = - 2\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the system using, for example, the elimination method. Notice that the $5y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-5y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}2x+\\cancel{5y} & =-16 \\\\ 7x-\\cancel{5y} & =-2 \\\\ \\hline 9x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $7x - 5y= -2$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-5y & =-2 \\\\ 7(-2)-5y & =-2 \\\\ -14-5y & =-2 \\\\ -5y & =12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2, \\,y=-\\dfrac{12}{5}.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186101440 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+6}{kx+2k}, & x\\ne -2 \\\\ k, & x=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186101440 1071 "Let $g(x)$ be the function defined below. For what values of $k$ is $g(x)$ continuous at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+6}{kx+2k}, & x\\ne -2 \\\\ k, & x=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186101932 1064 "Suppose we want to find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)\n$$ for the piecewise function given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an idea of the behavior of the function around $x=0,$ we can plot the function.\n\n![](images/78/78a832c65a4bb5862694717f52704a61660bd81a34eb7a72cfeeb98a52bb50f1.png)\n\nTo compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 0} f(x),\n$$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x < 0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3^{x+1}.$ We 