194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186134540 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}, & 0\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(1\\le X\\le 3).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186134541 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x/2|}}{4},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186134541 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x/2|}}{4},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186134541 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186134541 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/2}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186135049 1064 "We've already seen how to find the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace in the case when the subspace is defined using an orthogonal basis. In this lesson, we'll learn how to do the same thing in the case of non-orthogonal bases.\n\nFor example, consider the following vectors:\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\:\n\t\\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{x}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nHow can we find the orthogonal projection $\\mathbf{x}_S$ of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}?$ Notice that since $\\mathbf a_1\\cdot \\mathbf a_2 \\neq 0,$ the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are not orthogonal.\n\nAs usual, we would like to write $\\mathbf{x}$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\in S$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\!\\in S^\\perp.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a5576fcaf9e0be6fc14978eb91dcbaceee75cbfddaa537fdb05d34b8e31e2e0.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp,$ that is, $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\perp S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expression for $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ into the system to get\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( \\mathbf{x} - {k}_{1} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} - {k}_{2} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} ) \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} = 0 \\\\ ( \\mathbf{x} - {k}_{1} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} - {k}_{2} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} ) \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{k}_{1} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} + {k}_{2} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} = {\\color{red}( \\mathbf{x} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} \\\\ {k}_{1} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} + {k}_{2} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} = {\\color{red}( \\mathbf{x} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe can write this system in an equivalent matrix form as follows:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} - \\\\ - \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} -\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{k}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t=\n{\\color{red}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} - \\\\ - \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} -\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}| \\\\ \\mathbf{x} \\\\ |\\end{array} \\right]}\n}\n$$\n\nIf we denote \n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nand $\\mathbf{k}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{k}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\nA^TA\\mathbf{k} = A^T\\mathbf{x}\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{k}=(A^TA)^{-1}A^T\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nAlso, we note that  $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2$ can be expressed as $\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x}\t= A\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =Ak \\\\  & =A({A}^{T}A)^{-1}{A}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a formula for the orthogonal projection!\n\nNow, we return to our example. Using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 11\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: Pay attention to the order in which we carried out the multiplication. Carrying out the operations in the order shown above can save us a lot of work!" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186135049 1064 "We've already seen how to find the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace in the case when the subspace is defined using an orthogonal basis. In this lesson, we'll learn how to do the same thing in the case of non-orthogonal bases.\n\nFor example, consider the following vectors:\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\:\n\t\\mathbf{a}_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{x}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nHow can we find the orthogonal projection $\\mathbf{x}_S$ of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}?$ Notice that since $\\mathbf a_1\\cdot \\mathbf a_2 \\neq 0,$ the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are not orthogonal.\n\nAs usual, we would like to write $\\mathbf{x}$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\in S$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\!\\in S^\\perp.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a5576fcaf9e0be6fc14978eb91dcbaceee75cbfddaa537fdb05d34b8e31e2e0.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp,$ that is, $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\perp S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expression for $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ into the system to get\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}( \\mathbf{x} - {k}_{1} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} - {k}_{2} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} ) \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} = 0 \\\\ ( \\mathbf{x} - {k}_{1} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} - {k}_{2} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} ) \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} = 0\\end{array} \\right. }\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{k}_{1} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} + {k}_{2} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} = {\\color{red}( \\mathbf{x} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} )} \\\\ {k}_{1} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} + {k}_{2} {\\color{blue}( {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} = {\\color{red}( \\mathbf{x} \\cdot  {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} )} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe can write this system in an equivalent matrix form as follows:\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} - \\\\ - \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} -\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{k}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t=\n{\\color{red}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} - \\\\ - \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} -\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}| \\\\ \\mathbf{x} \\\\ |\\end{array} \\right]}\n}\n$$\n\nIf we denote \n$$\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}| & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nand $\\mathbf{k}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{k}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\nA^TA\\mathbf{k} = A^T\\mathbf{x}\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{k}=(A^TA)^{-1}A^T\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nAlso, we note that  $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2$ can be expressed as $\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x}\t= A\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =Ak \\\\  & =A({A}^{T}A)^{-1}{A}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a formula for the orthogonal projection!\n\nNow, we return to our example. Using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 11\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: Pay attention to the order in which we carried out the multiplication. Carrying out the operations in the order shown above can save us a lot of work!" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186135062 1064 "Consider the following data set:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$100$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$110$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$120$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$130$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 110 \\\\ 1 & 120 \\\\ 1 & 130\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 15\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186135062 1064 "Consider the following data set:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$100$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$110$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$120$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$130$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 110 \\\\ 1 & 120 \\\\ 1 & 130\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 15\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186135063 1064 "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Goods $(x)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}16 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the curve that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}22.5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the quadratic curve of best fit (regression curve) is \n\n$$\ny = 22.5 - 9x + 2x^2.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186135063 1064 "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Goods $(x)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{X}\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}}^{𝜷}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}16 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18\\end{array} \\right]}}^{\\mathbf{y}},\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the curve that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}22.5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the quadratic curve of best fit (regression curve) is \n\n$$\ny = 22.5 - 9x + 2x^2.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186135064 1064 "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 1 $(x_1)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 2 $(x_2)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186135064 1064 "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 1 $(x_1)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$1$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Good Type 2 $(x_2)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Profit $(y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186135098 1064 "Let's consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown below:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large A}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large Q}\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large R}\n$$\n\nSuppose also that we are given the following system of equations that connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHow do we find $R?$\nFirst, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\mathbf{q}}_{1} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} + 3 {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} - 2 {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\\mathbf{q}}_{2}\\end{array} \\right. }\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1} = {\\color{red}2} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} = {\\color{red}- 9} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\\color{blue}3} {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} = {\\color{red}8} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} - {\\color{blue}4} {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} + {\\color{purple}4} {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIn this new system, the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_1$ correspond to the first-row entries of $R,$ the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_2$ correspond to the second-row entries of $R,$ and the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_3$ correspond to the third-row entries of $R.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large A}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large Q}\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}- 9} & {\\color{red}8} \\\\ 0 & {\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}- 4} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\color{purple}4}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large R}.\n$$\n\nTo see why this is, we can take the transpose of both sides of $A=QR{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =QR \\\\ {A}^{T} & =(QR)^{T} \\\\ {A}^{T} & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T} \\\\ \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{bmatrix}}^{{A}^{T}} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{{R}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we were to expand this matrix product, we would obtain our system of equations." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186135098 1064 "Let's consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown below:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large A}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large Q}\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large R}\n$$\n\nSuppose also that we are given the following system of equations that connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow do we find $R?$\nFirst, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\mathbf{q}}_{1} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} + 3 {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} - 2 {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\\mathbf{q}}_{2}\\end{array} \\right. }\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1} = {\\color{red}2} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} = {\\color{red}- 9} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} + {\\color{blue}3} {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} = {\\color{red}8} {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} - {\\color{blue}4} {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} + {\\color{purple}4} {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nIn this new system, the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_1$ correspond to the first-row entries of $R,$ the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_2$ correspond to the second-row entries of $R,$ and the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_3$ correspond to the third-row entries of $R.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large A}\n\t=\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}| & | & | \\\\ {\\mathbf{q}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{q}}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large Q}\n\t\\overbrace{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}2} & {\\color{red}- 9} & {\\color{red}8} \\\\ 0 & {\\color{blue}3} & {\\color{blue}- 4} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\color{purple}4}\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t}^{\\Large R}.\n$$\n\nTo see why this is, we can take the transpose of both sides of $A=QR{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =QR \\\\ {A}^{T} & =(QR)^{T} \\\\ {A}^{T} & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T} \\\\ \\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{bmatrix}}^{{A}^{T}} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{{R}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we were to expand this matrix product, we would obtain our system of equations." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186135143 1064 "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and\n$$\nQ = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\tR = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & - 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is ${\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186135143 1064 "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and\n$$\nQ = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\tR = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & - 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\e