egin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le x,\\,\\,0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}, & 0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=x\\big]$ for $0 <     x <     2.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186162248 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x},\\, & x>0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186162248 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x},\\, & x>0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186162248 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x>0,$ we have $y>0.$\n\n![](images/8b/8b2d1487d180db1df306cd8c83088ef0334d29769bd7ef94eed7f75d4e6cedf7.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $y>0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4{e}^{-4\\cdot y/2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186162248 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x>0,$ we have $y>0.$\n\n![](images/8b/8b2d1487d180db1df306cd8c83088ef0334d29769bd7ef94eed7f75d4e6cedf7.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $y>0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4{e}^{-4\\cdot y/2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186162279 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{x}, & 1<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186162279 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{x}, & 1<x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ use the change-of-variables method to find the probability density function of $Y.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186162279 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{-1}(y)) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)={e}^{y}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({e}^{y})\\cdot |{e}^{y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}\\sqrt{{e}^{y}}\\cdot {e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{y/2}\\cdot {e}^{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{3y/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $1<x<4,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=4,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=0,\\,u(4)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{14}{e}^{3y/2}, & 0<y<\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186162279 1074 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡X.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{-1}(y)) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)={e}^{y}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-v