\\le \\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $y > 1,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({F}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right)\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{-(y-1)/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & \\,y>1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy (and often worthwhile) to check that this is a valid probability density function." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186163339 1064 "Suppose the continuous random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_X(x) =\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{e}^{- x} , \\hspace{1em} & x > 0 \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{1em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\nLet $Y= 1+2X.$ Let's use the distribution function method to find the probability density function of $Y.$\n\nFirst, note that $Y = 1+2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x > 0,$ we have $y > 1.$\n\n![](images/54/544e551755f4c8a575ef227b77fe520deec203f08654eec31109e496141aa91c.png)\n\nLet's consider $y > 1.$ We write down the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ and then use our transformation to isolate $X$ in the parentheses. This will allow us to write $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligne