sponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186167304 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[5\\in B]$ has $x=5$ and $A=B.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[5\\in B]={1}_{B}(5).\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186167304 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[5\\in B]$ has $x=5$ and $A=B.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[5\\in B]={1}_{B}(5).\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186167330 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1} & 7{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 3\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186167330 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1} & 7{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 3\\end{array}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 3\\end{array}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{7}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle [A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186167504 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen box. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{5,6,…,15\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<10).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{5,6,…,15\\},$ and the set $S=\\{5,6,…,15\\}$ has $|S|=15-5+1=11$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{11}, & x=5,6,…,15 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<10$ are $5,6,7,8,9.$ There are $5$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<10)=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{11}=\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186167504 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen box. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{5,6,…,15\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(X<10).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a se