o, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{56}, & x=100,101,…,155 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X<130$ are $100,101,…,129.$ There are $30$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X<130)=30\\cdot \\frac{1}{56}=\\frac{15}{28}.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186167990 1064 "Consider an **inhomogeneous** linear system of ordinary differential equations in component form:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={a}_{11}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{12}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={a}_{21}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{22}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we can package the unknown functions into vectors\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2} ( t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1}^{\\prime } ( t ) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\\prime } ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThen, the system can be written as\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),\n\\qquad\nA={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{g}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{f}_{1} ( t ) \\\\ {f}_{2} ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nOften, the **forcing functions** $f_1(t), f_2(t)$ are linear combinations of a few basic functions (such as $e^{3t},$ $t^2,$ or $\\sin t$).\nIn that case, it is useful to write $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ as a sum of those basic functions times constant vectors.\n\nFor example, let's write down the system of linear ODEs below in matrix form.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+8{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+10{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 8+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0