, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{w} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_1 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\nc_2 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}t \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{t} \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.98$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.61$</td>\