$\\lambda_1=5,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/43/43c6294fa7f68b21422eee71d35edd5f7880b3b3c9e23a2a8078090416cded05.png)" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186168007 1064 "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ngiven that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},$ respectively.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]} = \nc_1 e^{5t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]} + \nc_2 e^{2t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n\\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_1 e^{5t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{5t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c7/c7c824e97d40d2f982525040d5f0ba697c34c40ff51700ec3952d2909d566e21.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_2 e^{2t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c3/c3030613b03a1afabd964d9494bf2359bd6ce5f021c731feda9607a81aaf5df6.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n0 < \\lambda_2 < \\lambda_1\n$$\n\nthus, $\\lambda_1 = 5$ is the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=5,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/43/43c6294fa7f68b21422eee71d35edd5f7880b3b3c9e23a2a8078090416cded05.png)" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186172612 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 0\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186172612 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186172619 1074 "Since the earthquakes occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next earthquake using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next earthquake. Since there is an earthquake every $2.5$ months on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2.5}=2/5$ earthquakes per month.\n\nThe number of earthquakes can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next earthquake will not occur for at least $6$ months is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-2x/5}|}_{6}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ={e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ≈0.0907.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186172619 1074 "Since the earthquakes occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next earthquake using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next earthquake. Since there is an earthquake every $2.5$ months on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2.5}=2/5$ earthquakes per month.\n\nThe number of earthquakes can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next earthquake will not occur for at least $6$ months is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-2x/5}|}_{6}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ={e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ≈0.0907.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186172635 1074 "Since the errors occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next error using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next error. The time until the next error can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since errors occur once every $30$ days on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{30}\\right).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that first error will be registered between $2$ and $12$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<12) & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/30}{|}_{2}^{12} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/5}+{e}^{-1/15} \\\\  & ≈0.2652\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186172635 1074 "Since the errors occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next error using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next error. The time until the next error can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since errors occur once every $30$ days on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{30}\\right).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that first error will be registered between $2$ and $12$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<12) & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/30}{|}_{2}^{12} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/5}+{e}^{-1/15} \\\\  & ≈0.2652\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186172637 1074 "Since the time until the next call is answered occurs continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, between calls. Then, since there are $3$ calls per minute on average, the rate is $\\lambda =3$ calls per minute.\n\nThe amount of time before the next call can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =3,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next call will occur after $1$ minute or more is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.0498.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186172637 1074 "Since the time until the next call is answered occurs continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, between calls. Then, since there are $3$ calls per minute on average, the rate is $\\lambda =3$ calls per minute.\n\nThe amount of time before the next call can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =3,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next call will occur after $1$ minute or more is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.0498.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186173153 1064 "In general, the cross-term of a quadratic equation can be eliminated by an orthogonal change of variables. That is, one of the kind \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n  where the columns of $P$ form an orthonormal  eigenbasis for the corresponding quadratic form's matrix.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C$ be a plane curve defined as \n\n$$\n\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1x_2 -2\\sqrt2x_1+2\\sqrt2x_2 =8.\n$$\n\nHow do we determine the type of our curve?\n\nNotice that the quadratic form $2x_1x_2$ can be reduced to canonical form $y_1^2-y_2^2$ using the change of variables\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}}\\end{array} \\right]} \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]} = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}}\\end{array} \\right]} \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}{x}_{1} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} {y}_{1} - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} {y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} {y}_{1} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} {y}_{2} ,\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nWe know that this change of variables reduces the corresponding quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = 2x_1x_2$ to its canonical form. Indeed, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=8\\,⟹\\,{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{2}=8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n and, by completing the  square for the variable $y_2$,  we get the equation \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the resulting equation in the $y_1,y_2$ variables is the following.\n\n![](images/d5/d532f5207a9d518f92024c86689285834b86c961d0f647d6ee99ad6dd9c7c329.png)\n\nNotice that we can write down the change-of-variables matrix as\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\cos   {45}^{\\circ } & - \\sin   {45}^{\\circ } \\\\ \\sin   {45}^{\\circ } & \\cos   {45}^{\\circ }\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nwhich is a rotation matrix. Looking at the diagram above, we see that going from one coordinate system to another can be viewed as a rotation of our axes by $45^\\circ.$\n\n**Watch out!** In general, this action is an orthogonal transformation (it can be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of such transformations).\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}-2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis (standard) equation represents a hyperbola in which  both semiaxes are equal to $2.$      \n\nIn the diagram below we represent the curve in the $z_1,z_2$ variables.\n\n![](images/c3/c36709f752bd9c319662e7e2150c54031cc8fb3934be37711c65e7a7668829ca.png)\n\nLooking at the diagram above, we see that in the final step we simply translated our system by $(0,2)$ in $y$-coordinates.\n\n**Note:** Finding the simplest equation of a quadratic curve can be viewed as the following series of steps:\n\n- **Step 1**. We eliminate the cross-term by reducing the quadratic part to canonical form. Geometrically, this is equivalent to an orthogonal transformation of our coordinate system.\n\n- **Step 2**. We eliminate the linear terms by completing the squares. Geometrically, this is equivalent to a translation of our coordinate system." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186173153 1064 "In general, the cross-term of a quadratic equation can be eliminated by an orthogonal change of variables. That is, one of the kind \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n  where the columns of $P$ form an orthonormal  eigenbasis for the corresponding quadratic form's matrix.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C$ be a plane curve defined as \n\n$$\n\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1x_2 -2\\sqrt2x_1+2\\sqrt2x_2 =8.\n$$\n\nHow do we determine the type of our curve?\n\nNotice that the quadratic form $2x_1x_2$ can be reduced to canonical form $y_1^2-y_2^2$ using the change of variables\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]} =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}} \\\\ {\\displays