refore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186193073 1071 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[5X-2].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{3}, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}, & 1\\le x<3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186193073 1071 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[5X-2].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{3}, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}, & 1\\le x<3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186199015 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y}{2}=z+4=t\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=t-4,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+2(2t)^{2} & =(t-4)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+8{t}^{2} & ={t}^{2}-8t+16 \\\\ 8{t}^{2}+8t-16 & =0 \\\\ 8({t}^{2}+t-2) & =0 \\\\ 8(t+2)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ y=2(-2)=-4 \\\\ z=-2-4=-6,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-2,-4,-6).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=1-4=-3,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,2,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-6)$ and $(1,2,-3).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186199015 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y}{2}=z+4=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=t-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+2(2t)^{2} & =(t-4)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+8{t}^{2} & ={t}^{2}-8t+16 \\\\ 8{t}^{2}+8t-16 & =0 \\\\ 8({t}^{2}+t-2) & =0 \\\\ 8(t+2)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ y=2(-2)=-4 \\\\ z=-2-4=-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-2,-4,-6).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=1-4=-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,2,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-6)$ and $(1,2,-3).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186199484 1064 "Suppose we want to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \\leq 1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac34145b3911b19eb3775a915e8f0462c78502be6d6292e7682265c86eafce7.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find the extrema, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the critical points of $f$ inside the domain $x^2+y^2 < 1.$\n\n- Then, we find the extreme values of $f$ subject to the constraint $x^2+y^2=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\n- Finally, we determine the maximum and minimum values by evaluating $f$ at the candidate points.\n\nRecall that the critical points of a function $f(x,y)$ are the solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ Now, since the derivative of $f$ is the transpose of its gradient $\\nabla f,$ the critical points are also given by the solutions to $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0.$\n\nSo, we can find the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ Note that this point belongs to our domain.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + y^2  = 1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1), \\qquad (0,0).\n$$\n\nNote that we add the critical point $(0,0)$ to the list of the candidates above.\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points: \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$(x,y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(\\pm 1,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0, \\pm 1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0, 0)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x,y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $0$ at the point $(0, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at the points $(0, \\pm 1).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186199484 1064 "Suppose we want to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \\leq 1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac34145b3911b19eb3775a915e8f0462c78502be6d6292e7682265c86eafce7.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find the extrema, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the critical points of $f$ inside the domain $x^2+y^2 < 1.$\n\n- Then, we find the extreme values of $f$ subject to the constraint $x^2+y^2=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\n- Finally, we determine the maximum and minimum values by evaluating $f$ at the candidate points.\n\nRecall that the critical points of a function $f(x,y)$ are the solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ Now, since the derivative of $f$ is the transpose of its gradient $\\nabla f,$ the critical points are also given by the solutions to $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0.$\n\nSo, we can find the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ Note that this point belongs to our domain.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + y^2 