/tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $0$ at the point $(0, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at the points $(0, \\pm 1).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186199500 1064 "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = -y = -z+1.\n$$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx = -y = -z+1 = t  \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = t \\\\ y = - t \\\\ z = - t + 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $$\n\\displaystyle (\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})\n$$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186199500 1064 "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = -y = -z+1.\n$$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx = -y = -z+1 = t  \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = t \\\\ y = - t \\\\ z = - t + 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $$\n\\displaystyle (\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})\n$$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186216959 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ y=3x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2 & =3x \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x_1 = 1$ and $x_2 = 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 = 1 + 2 = 3.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186216959 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ y=3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2 & =3x \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x_1 = 1$ and $x_2 = 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 = 1 + 2 = 3.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186216961 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+6x \\\\ y=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x & =9 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+6x-9 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-6x+9) & =0 \\\\ -(x-3)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=3.$ \n\nWe know the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(3, 9).$ Therefore, $p=3,\\; q=9,$ and\n\n$$\np+q = 3+9 = 12.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/f9/f9d09e07988752af26143fa2dc8df260784be5fcb16c28d8115bb44fc3c1a4a1.png)" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186216961 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+6x \\\\ y=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x & =9 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+6x-9 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-6x+9) & =0 \\\\ -(x-3)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=3.$ \n\nWe know the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(3, 9).$ Therefore, $p=3,\\; q=9,$ and\n\n$$\np+q = 3+9 = 12.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/f9/f9d09e07988752af26143fa2dc8df260784be5fcb16c28d8115bb44fc3c1a4a1.png)" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186216962 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=x +2k$ intersects the parabola $y=x^2-x+3,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-x+3 \\\\ y=x+2k\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+3 & =x+2k \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+3-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\, b=-2,$ and $c=3-2k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3-2k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4-12+8k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{8k-8}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8k-8 & <0 \\\\ 8k & <8 \\\\ k & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k \\lt   1.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186216962 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=x +2k$ intersects the parabola $y=x^2-x+3,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-x+3 \\\\ y=x+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+3 & =x+2k \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+3-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\, b=-2,$ and $c=3-2k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3-2k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4-12+8k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{8k-8}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8k-8 & <0 \\\\ 8k & <8 \\\\ k & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k \\lt   1.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186218320 1064 "Consider the line $y = x + 2$ and the parabola $y = x^2 + x + 1,$ shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/9d/9d7ca10163c9b5096f90e19bdb50d98881bd55e92cbaea4aa5af6696e728f9f3.png)\n\nWe can see from the diagram that there are two points of intersection. So let's calculate the coordinates of these intersection points.\n\nAn intersection point is a common solution of the equations $y = x + 2$ and $y = x^2 + x + 1.$ In other words, to get the coordinates of the intersection points, we have to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x+1 \\\\ y=x+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and then solving for $x$ using our knowledge of quadratic equations, we get\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {x}^{2} + x + 1 & \\displaystyle  = x + 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle {x}^{2} + \\cancel{x} + 1 & \\displaystyle  = \\cancel{x} + 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle {x}^{2} + 1 & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle {x}^{2} - 1 & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle ( x + 1 ) ( x - 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle x & \\displaystyle  = - 1 , 1.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting $x=−1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,1).$\n\n- Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =(1)+2 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the points of intersection are $\\left(-1,\\,1 \\right)$ and $\\left(1,\\,3 \\right).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186218320 1064 "Consider the line $y = x + 2$ and the parabola $y = x^2 + x + 1,$ shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/9d/9d7ca10163c9b5096f90e19bdb50d98881bd55e92cbaea4aa5af6696e728f9f3.png)\n\nWe can see from the diagram that there are two points of intersection. So let's calculate the coordinates of these intersection points.\n\nAn intersection point is a common solution of the equations $y = x + 2$ and $y = x^2 + x + 1.$ In other words, to get the coordinates of the intersection points, we have to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x+1 \\\\ y=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and then solving for $x$ using our knowledge of quadratic equations, we get\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {x}^{2} + x + 1 & \\displaystyle  = x + 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle {x}^{2} + \\cancel{x} + 1 & \\displaystyle  = \\cancel{x} + 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle {x}^{2} + 1 & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\\\ \\displaystyle {x}^{2} - 1 & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle ( x + 1 ) ( x - 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle x & \\displaystyle  = - 1 , 1.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting $x=−1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,1).$\n\n- Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =(1)+2 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the points of intersection are $\\left(-1,\\,1 \\right)$ and $\\left(1,\\,3 \\right).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186221952 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186221952 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186221952 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/309cbea2fa350c44c7a9225ec046b827113f9ffaa1dea6d746ab097e66cf945a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+2 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d6/d6ec97d8fc3afffe41dca1dfd9d1918de586220408584183ef2eaad52e502485.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-2 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/09/0990ae7edeef543034fad206797305c41378b8243f809f5914f67c1e3b3955aa.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/af/af8a897bcc199d1f07332d6d8dd26cd7d6173943378777d72246909e89d7837b.png)" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186221952 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/309cbea2fa350c44c7a9225ec046b827113f9ffaa1dea6d746ab097e66cf945a.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+2 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d6/d6ec97d8fc3afffe41dca1dfd9d1918de586220408584183ef2eaad52e502485.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-2 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/09/0990ae7edeef543034fad206797305c41378b8243f809f5914f67c1e3b3955aa.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/af/af8a897bc