rue] [17592186262576 1071 "Compute $P(X\\ge 3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186262579 1071 "Compute $P(X>2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186262579 1071 "Compute $P(X>2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186265765 1071 "![](images/b9/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186265765 1071 "![](images/b9/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186265765 1074 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$ and $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$ do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$ only." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186265765 1074 "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$ and $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$ do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$ only." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186265783 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/89/8944afb608465e49c7ae253f8e2518aa3d43b1c6e121dd91178ca1a67116a508.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$ Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ equals $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186265783 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/89/8944afb608465e49c7ae253f8e2518aa3d43b1c6e121dd91178ca1a67116a508.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}.\n$$ Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$ equals $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$ only." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186265803 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{