{x}^{2}+2),\\, & -2\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & 1<x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186419955 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{108}x(x+2),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186419955 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{108}x(x+2),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186419955 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0\\le x\\le 6,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1, & x>6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{108}t(t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{324}{t}^{2}(t+3)|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{324}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{3}}{324}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{108}, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1, & x>6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186419955 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0\\le x\\le 6,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1, & x>6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{108}t(t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{324}{t}^{2}(t+3)|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{324}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{3}}{324}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{108}, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1, & x>6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186420184 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}{e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}{e}^{(-3)^{2}}=9{e}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186420184 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}{e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}{e}^{(-3)^{2}}=9{e}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186420398 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,