0 1074 "Since arrivals of vehicles at the crossing occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next vehicle arrives as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing. Then, since vehicles arrive every $10$ seconds on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ vehicles per second.\n\nThe time between arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing at least $5$ seconds after the previous vehicle's arrival is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/10}{|}_{5}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-5/10} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186435502 1074 "Since the lifetime of the switches varies continuously, lifetimes of different switches are independent, and switches replacements have a constant average rate, we can model the lifetime of a switch using the exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the lifetime, in years, of a random electrical switch. The lifetime can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since switches are replaced every $2$ years on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a switch fails between the first and the second year is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<2) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.2387\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186435502 1074 "Since the lifetime of the switches varies continuously, lifetimes of different switches are independent, and switches replacements have a constant average rate, we can model the lifetime of a switch using the exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the lifetime, in years, of a random electrical switch. The lifetime can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since switches are replaced every $2$ years on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a switch fails between the first and the second year is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<2) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.2387\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186435806 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The argument of the arccosine has values between $-1$ and $1,$ and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1<\\frac{1}{y-x}<1 \\\\ y-x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le \\frac{1}{y-x}\\le 1,\\,y-x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186435806 1074 "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The argument of the arccosine has values between $-1$ and $1,$ and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-1<\\frac{1}{y-x}<1 \\\\ y-x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le \\frac{1}{y-x}\\le 1,\\,y-x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186436042 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186436042 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{si