x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,2]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,2]$” only." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186436280 1071 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Give your answer rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-1, & 2<x<3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{12}, & 3\\le x<4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186436280 1071 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Give your answer rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-1, & 2<x<3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{12}, & 3\\le x<4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186436282 1071 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variabl