e probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-|5x|},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186442323 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-5t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186442323 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-5t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186442324 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{8}{9},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186442324 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{8}{9},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186442324 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}(-\\frac{2}{9}t+\\frac{8}{9})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={(-\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}t)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}x-\\frac{7}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}({x}^{2}-8x+7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7), & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186442324 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}(-\\frac{2}{9}t+\\frac{8}{9})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={(-\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}t)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}x-\\frac{7}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}({x}^{2}-8x+7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7), & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186442325 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{64}x(16-{x}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186442325 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{64}x(16-{x}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186442325 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability density function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0<x<4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{64}t(16-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{64}(8{t}^{2}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}(8{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{a