et $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ g(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186476875 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ g(x), & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186476875 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}1\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =[u]_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =x-0 \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ x, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)=x.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186476875 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the marginal CDF of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{f}_{X}(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}1\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =[u]_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =x-0 \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${F}_{X}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ x, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $g(x)=x.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186476879 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}c\\sqrt{x-y},\\, & 0\\le x-{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186476879 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c\\sqrt{x-y},\\, & 0\\le x-{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186476882 1071 "Find $\\text{E}[XY],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \