.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1335\\,$ | $\\,0.1314\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ | $\\,0.1515\\,$ |" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186500293 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1458}, & 0\\le x\\le 9 \\\\ 1, & x>9,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nfind $P(1<X\\le 3).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186500293 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1458}, & 0\\le x\\le 9 \\\\ 1, & x>9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(1<X\\le 3).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186500304 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2(-x+4),\\, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<3 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186500304 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2(-x+4),\\, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<3 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186500304 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $3\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{x}2(-t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={8t-{t}^{2}|}_{3}^{x} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+8x-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-15, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186500304 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $3\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{x}2(-t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={8t-{t}^{2}|}_{3}^{x} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+8x-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-15, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186501183 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75}x,\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<5 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186501183 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75}x,\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<5 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186501183 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $5\\le x\\le 10,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<5 \\\\ {\\int }_{5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 1, & x>10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{5}^{x}\\frac{2}{75}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{75}{t}^{2}|}_{5}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{75}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<5, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{75},\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186501183 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $5\\le x\\le 10,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<5 \\\\ {\\int }_{5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 1, & x>10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{5}^{x}\\frac{2}{75}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{75}{t}^{2}|}_{5}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{75}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<5, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{75},\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186501225 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x|}}{2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186501225 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x|}}{2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186501225 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2},\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186501225 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2},\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186502090 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $q$ and $t$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{q,t\\}.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186502090 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $q$ and $t$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{q,t\\}.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186502110 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{3,5\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed92f46f57aefc4d9c7445f044f1b35adb89950c16f331aff35c02445b607c95.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $3$ and $5$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$" 13194139544833 175921865764