54 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $y=z,$ any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $$\n\\displaystyle Tx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has no solution, this means that $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\notin \\text{Im}(T).\n$$ Therefore, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\}=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186505754 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $y=z,$ any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $$\n\\displaystyle Tx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has no solution, this means that $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\notin \\text{Im}(T).\n$$ Therefore, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\}=\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186505938 1080 "f(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1,\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ -2-2x,