4139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186532620 1071 "Suppose we would like to find the minimum of the function $f(x,y,z)=xy-zy$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y+z=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,-y⟩=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,⟩ \\\\ y+z=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186532620 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy-zy\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186532620 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy-zy\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,2y,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186532628 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.63),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.37)}^{x-1}(0.63), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(0.37)}^{0}(0.63)+{(0.37)}^{1}(0.63)+{(0.37)}^{2}(0.63) \\\\  & ≈0.9493.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186532628 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.63),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.37)}^{x-1}(0.63), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(0.37)}^{0}(0.63)+{(0.37)}^{1}(0.63)+{(0.37)}^{2}(0.63) \\\\  & ≈0.9493.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186532641 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} \\\\ y=9x-14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =9x-14 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9x+14 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-7) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=7.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the parabola since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)^{2} \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,4).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2} \\\\  & =49,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,49).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4e0ce399e32d7b957b71384d61cf08024b5d9995fde28d5f1fac71e1f123b2aa.png)" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186532641 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} \\\\ y=9x-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =9x-14 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9x+14 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-7) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=7.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the parabola since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)^{2} \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,4).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2} \\\\  & =49,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,49).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4e0ce399e32d7b957b71384d61cf08024b5d9995fde28d5f1fac71e1f123b2aa.png)" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186532663 1074 "We need to solve the following system consi