ght.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 \\\\ -4x+6y=-8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{4x}-8y-400 & =0 \\\\ -\\cancel{4x}+6y & =-8 \\\\ \\hline (-8y+6y)-400 & =-8 \\\\ -2y & =392 \\\\ y & =-196\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-196$ into the equation $2x-3y=4$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =4 \\\\ 2x-3(-196) & =4 \\\\ 2x+588 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =-584 \\\\ x & =-292\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-292$ and $y=-196.$ So, \n\n$$\nx-y=-292-(-196)=-292+196=-96.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186535991 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the top equation by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.04x-0.08y-4=0 & |\\,\\times 100 \\\\ 2x-3y=4 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 \\\\ 2x-3y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ we will get a $-4x$ term in the second equation that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 &  \\\\ 2x-3y=4 & |\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 \\\\ -4x+6y=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{4x}-8y-400 & =0 \\\\ -\\cancel{4x}+6y & =-8 \\\\ \\hline (-8y+6y)-400 & =-8 \\\\ -2y & =392 \\\\ y & =-196\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-196$ into the equation $2x-3y=4$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =4 \\\\ 2x-3(-196) & =4 \\\\ 2x+588 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =-584 \\\\ x & =-292\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-292$ and $y=-196.$ So, \n\n$$\nx-y=-292-(-196)=-292+196=-96.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186536000 1071 "Let $(x,y)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $x+y?$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05x-0.03y=0.04 \\\\ 0.4x+0.2y=0.1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186536000 1071 "Let $(x,y)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $x+y?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05x-0.03y=0.04 \\\\ 0.4x+0.2y=0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186536000 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the first equations by $100$ and the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05x-0.03y=0.04 & |\\times 100 \\\\ 0.4x+0.2y=0.1 & |\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y=4 \\\\ 4x+2y=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $3,$ we will get a $-6y$ term in the first equation that can cancel with the $6y$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y=4 & |\\,\\times (2) \\\\ 4x+2y=1 & |\\,\\times (3)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-6y=8 \\\\ 12x+6y=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}10x-\\cancel{6y} & =8 \\\\ 12x+\\cancel{6y} & =3 \\\\ \\hline (10x+12x) & =(8+3) \\\\ 22x & =11 \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{22} \\\\ x & =0.5\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=0.5$ into the equation $4x+2y=1$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2y & =1 \\\\ 4(0.5)+2y & =1 \\\\ 2+2y & =1 \\\\ 2y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=0.5$ and $y=-0.5.$ So, \n\n$$\nx+y=0.5+(-0.5)=0.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186536000 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the first equations by $100$ and the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05x-0.03y=0.04 & |\\times 100 \\\\ 0.4x+0.2y=0.1 & |\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y=4 \\\\ 4x+2y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $3,$ we will get a $-6y$ term in the first equation that can cancel with the $6y$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y=4 & |\\,\\times (2) \\\\ 4x+2y=1 & |\\,\\times (3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-6y=8 \\\\ 12x+6y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}10x-\\cancel{6y} & =8 \\\\ 12x+\\cancel{6y} & =3 \\\\ \\hline (10x+12x) & =(8+3) \\\\ 22x & =11 \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{22} \\\\ x & =0.5\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=0.5$ into the equation $4x+2y=1$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2y & =1 \\\\ 4(0.5)+2y & =1 \\\\ 2+2y & =1 \\\\ 2y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=0.5$ and $y=-0.5.$ So, \n\n$$\nx+y=0.5+(-0.5)=0.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186536843 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about $O$ can be represented by \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(-y,x).\n$$\n\nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(2,3)↦(-3,2).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the translation to $(-3,2),$ we get\n\n$$\n(-3,2)↦(-3-p,2+q).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(-1,5),$ by equating the $x$- and $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3-p=-1 \\\\ 2+q=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p=-2 \\\\ q=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\np+q=(-2)+3=1.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186536843 1074 "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA rotation of ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about $O$ can be represented by \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(-y,x).\n$$\n\nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(2,3)↦(-3,2).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the translation to $(-3,2),$ we get\n\n$$\n(-3,2)↦(-3-p,2+q).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(-1,5),$ by equating the $x$- and $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-3-p=-1 \\\\ 2+q=5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}p=-2 \\\\ q=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\np+q=(-2)+3=1.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186537893 1071 "![](images/e1/e1892e632e2485b17400ba9d2a9372bf164e20812c3b6fe4f9b03ab1348fab7d.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y=f(x) \\\\ y=g(x)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186537893 1071 "![](images/e1/e1892e632e2485b17400ba9d2a9372bf164e20812c3b6fe4f9b03ab1348fab7d.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=f(x) \\\\ y=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186537980 1071 "![](images/e6/e6831eff5d8feb87ebba8e7d449d98fbe72c3ed4df01d8374d4866e8e3456ddc.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+1 \\\\ y={x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186537980 1071 "![](images/e6/e6831eff5d8feb87ebba8e7d449d98fbe72c3ed4df01d8374d4866e8e3456ddc.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+1 \\\\ y={x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186538936 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-12y \\\\  & =12(2{x}^{2}-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =-12x+3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3({y}^{2}-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨12(2{x}^{2}-y),\\,3({y}^{2}-4x),\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}12(2{x}^{2}-y)=0 \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4x)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=2{x}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({(2{x}^{2})}^{2}-4x) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{4}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of the last equation are $x=0,1.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({0}^{3})-12(0)(0)+{0}^{3}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our first point is $(0,0,1).$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({1}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({1}^{3})-12(1)(2)+{2}^{3}+1=-7.\n$$\n\nSo, our second point is $(1,2,-7).$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186538936 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-12y \\\\  & =12(2{x}^{2}-y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{x}^{3}-12xy+{y}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =-12x+3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3({y}^{2}-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨12(2{x}^{2}-y),\\,3({y}^{2}-4x),\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}12(2{x}^{2}-y)=0 \\\\ 3({y}^{2}-4x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=2{x}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({(2{x}^{2})}^{2}-4x) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{4}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of the last equation are $x=0,1.$ So, we have two cases.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({0}^{3})-12(0)(0)+{0}^{3}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our first point is $(0,0,1).$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we have \n\n$$\ny=2({1}^{2})=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=8({1}^{3})-12(1)(2)+{2}^{3}+1=-7.\n$$\n\nSo, our second point is $(1,2,-7).$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(0,0,1)$ and $(1,2,-7).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186538983 1074 "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{8}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation,