ction is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x,\\, & x\\le 4 \\\\ 2,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186555833 1074 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,4],$ we have a line with a slope of $1$ and $y$-intercept $0.$ So the graph is the line $y=x$ that ends at $(4,4),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(4,\\infty ),$ we have a horizontal line that passes through the point $(4,2).$ So the graph is the line $y=2$ that starts at $(4,2),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x,\\, & x\\le 4 \\\\ 2,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186555835 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=120$ from a population where $5\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,120.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{120}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186555835 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=120$ from a population where $5\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,120.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{120}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 1319