 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186599805 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{(x-1)({e}^{y}-1)}{x{e}^{y}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{e}^{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{e}^{y}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186599805 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{(x-1)({e}^{y}-1)}{x{e}^{y}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{e}^{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{e}^{y}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186599834 1071 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x({y