he corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-6x})(1-{e}^{-y/3}) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-y/3}-{e}^{-6x}+{e}^{-6x-y/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-y/3}-{e}^{-6x}+{e}^{-6x-y/3}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186599842 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-6x})(1-{e}^{-y/3}) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-y/3}-{e}^{-6x}+{e}^{-6x-y/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-y/3}-{e}^{-6x}+{e}^{-6x-y/3}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186601893 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y-1}{2}, & \\,0\\le 3x\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{7}{16},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{39}{16},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2421,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.3655,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186601893 1071 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y-1}{2}, & \\,0\\le 3x\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\