nce $\\text{nullity}(A-4I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A-4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A-4I$ is singular, then $(A-4I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186613134 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-4I) & =2-\\text{rank}(A-4I) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-4I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A-4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A-4I$ is singular, then $(A-4I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186613147 1074 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,1],$ we have a line with a slope of $-1$ and $y$-intercept $4.$ So the graph is the line $y=-x+4$ that ends at $(1,3),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(1,\\infty )$, we have a line with a slope of $1$ that passes through the point $(1,1).$ We can find the equation of this line using point-slope form:\n\n$$\ny-1=1(x-1)\\,⟹\\,y=x\n$$\n\nSo the graph is the line $y=x$ that starts at $(1,1),$