(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-x/9}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(10\\le X<15)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10\\le X<15) & ={\\int }_{10}^{15}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{10}^{15}\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-x/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/9}{|}_{10}^{15} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5/3}+{e}^{-10/9} \\\\  & ≈0.1403\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186613736 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-x/9}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(10\\le X<15)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10\\le X<15) & ={\\int }_{10}^{15}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{10}^{15}\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-x/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/9}{|}_{10}^{15} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-5/3}+{e}^{-10/9} \\\\  & ≈0.1403\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186613751 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}), & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186613751 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density fun