ise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186622783 1074 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}(y-1)}{y}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186622811 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{1-x}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186622811 1071 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{1-x}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}, & y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186622811 1074 "If 