e joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(x+y)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind $P(-1\\le X<10).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186635493 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(x+y)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(-1\\le X<10).$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186635493 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(x+y)}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-(x+y)}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty } \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-(x+0)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1\\le X<10) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{10}{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{10}{e}^{-x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-x}]}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-10}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186635493 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) &={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $x\\ge 0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(x+y)}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-(x+y)}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty } \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-(x+0)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1\\le X<10) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{10}{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{10}{e}^{-x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-x}]}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-10}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186635497 1074 "An exponential random variable $W$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda w}, & w\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nW∼\\text{Exp}(0.5).\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0.5{e}^{-0.5w}, & w\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(2\\le W\\le 4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le W\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}f(w)\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}0.5{e}^{-0.5w}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.5w}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈0.2325\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186635497 1074 "An exponential random variable $W$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda w}, & w\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nW∼\\text{Exp}(0.5).\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(w)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.5{e}^{-0.5w}, & w\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe