 marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{5,6,…,19\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(7<X\\le 10).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{5,6,…,19\\},$ and the set $S=\\{5,6,…,19\\}$ has $|S|=19-5+1=15$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{15}, & x=5,6,…,19 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $7<X\\le 10$ are $8,9,10.$ There are $3$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(7<X\\le 10)=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{15}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186646269 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{5,6,…,19\\},$ and we wish to compute $P(7<X\\le 10).$\n\nIn general, if $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{5,6,…,19\\},$ and the set $S=\\{5,6,…,19\\}$ has $|S|=19-5+1=15$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{15}, & x=5,6,…,19 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $7<X\\le 10$ are $8,9,10.$ There are $3$ of these elements. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(7<X\\le 10)=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{15}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186651596 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{b,c,d\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4afa52dee6ffc5c42d95d684871dbedfb1445bbce5eabc6b9319e97ae6dafd2.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $b,c$ and $d$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186651596 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{b,c,d\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4afa52dee6ffc5c42d95d684871dbedfb1445bbce5eabc6b9319e97ae6dafd2.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $b,c$ and $d$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186651613 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $0,4,6$ and $8$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{0,4,6,8\\}.$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186651613 1074 "An indicator function maps 