le $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-10,10],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20},\\, & -10\\le x\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X<4) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4-(-2)}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186653539 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-10,10],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{20},\\, & -10\\le x\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X<4) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{1}{20}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4-(-2)}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186653544 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\sqrt{2},0],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\, & -\\sqrt{2}\\le x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge -1) & =P(-1\\le X\\le 0) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(0-(-1))\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186653544 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\sqrt{2},0],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\, & -\\sqrt{2}\\le x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge -1) & =P(-1\\le X\\le 0) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(0-(-1))\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186653550 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & -\\frac{9}{2}\\le x\\le -\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-1) & =P(-\\frac{9}{2}\\le X<-1) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-9/2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-9/2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{[-1-\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)]}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186653550 1074 "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,