ute $P(7\\le X<10).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[5,12],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7}, & 5\\le x\\le 12 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7\\le X<10) & ={\\int }_{7}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{7}^{10}\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{7}|}_{7}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{7}-\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 true] [17592186658980 1074 "Let $X$ represent the weight lost during the first month for a randomly chosen person following the diet. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[5,12],$ and we wish to compute $P(7\\le X<10).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[5,12],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7}, & 5\\le x\\le 12 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7\\le X<10) & ={\\int }_{7}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{7}^{10}\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{7}|}_{7}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{7}-\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544833 17592186576411 false] [17592186658983 1074 "Notice that the coefficient of $p$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $5.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-5,$ then the coefficient of $p$ will turn into $-5,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $5$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(p+q=6)\\,\\times (-5) \\